---
import FinanceLayout from "../../../../components/finance/FinanceLayout.astro";

const ArrowLeft = `<path d="m12 19-7-7 7-7"></path><path d="M19 12H5"></path>`;
---

<FinanceLayout title="Roth Conversion Strategy for Seattle Residents | WAFS CPA" description="A Seattle CPA's guide to Roth conversion — when to convert, tax implications, and how Washington State's no-income-tax advantage affects your Roth strategy.">
	<!-- Hero -->
	<section class="relative bg-wafs-forest pt-32 pb-16 text-warmwhite">
		<div class="absolute inset-0 z-0">
			<img src="/images/finance/hero-seattle.png" alt="Seattle skyline" class="h-full w-full object-cover opacity-60" />
			<div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-wafs-forest via-wafs-forest/45 to-transparent"></div>
		</div>
		<div class="relative z-10 container mx-auto px-6">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
				<a href="/finance/resources/blog" class="mb-8 flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-medium text-warmwhite/60 transition-colors hover:text-wafs-gold">
					<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4 rotate-180" set:html={ArrowLeft} /> Back to Blog
				</a>
				<div>
					<p class="mb-4 font-mono text-sm tracking-wider text-wafs-gold uppercase">January 15, 2025</p>
					<h1 class="font-display mb-8 text-4xl leading-tight font-semibold md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl">Roth Conversion Strategy in Seattle: 2025 Guide</h1>
					<div class="flex items-center gap-4 border-t border-white/10 pt-8">
						<img src="/images/finance/team-robert.png" alt="Robert Kimura" class="h-12 w-12 rounded-full object-cover grayscale" />
						<div>
							<p class="font-semibold">Robert Kimura, CPA, CFP®</p>
							<p class="text-sm text-warmwhite/60">Partner — Wealth & Financial Planning</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<!-- Body -->
	<section class="bg-warmwhite py-12">
		<div class="container mx-auto px-6">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
				<img src="/images/finance/blog-roth.png" alt="Retirement planning charts" class="mb-16 aspect-[2/1] w-full rounded-sm object-cover shadow-md" />

				<div class="prose-wafs max-w-none">
					<p class="mb-8 text-xl leading-relaxed text-wafs-forest italic">A Roth conversion is one of the most powerful wealth-building tools available to high-net-worth investors, but executing it poorly can trigger massive, irreversible tax consequences. Because we operate as both CPAs and CFPs, we view Roth conversions through a dual lens.</p>

					<h2 class="font-display mt-12 mb-4 text-3xl font-semibold text-wafs-forest">The Mechanics of a Roth Conversion</h2>
					<p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed text-[#4a5568]">At its core, a Roth conversion involves moving funds from a traditional, pre-tax retirement account (like a Traditional IRA or 401k) into a post-tax Roth IRA.</p>
					<p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed text-[#4a5568]">
						<strong class="font-semibold text-wafs-forest">The Catch:</strong> The amount you convert is added to your ordinary income for the year, subject to federal income tax.<br />
						<strong class="font-semibold text-wafs-forest">The Reward:</strong> Once the money is in the Roth IRA, it grows completely tax-free forever, and all qualified withdrawals are tax-free. Furthermore, Roth IRAs are not subject to Required Minimum Distributions (RMDs) during your lifetime.
					</p>

					<h2 class="font-display mt-12 mb-4 text-3xl font-semibold text-wafs-forest">The "Valley of Lower Income" Strategy</h2>
					<p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed text-[#4a5568]">The mathematical goal of a Roth conversion is simple: pay taxes on the money now at a lower tax rate than you expect to pay in the future.</p>
					<p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed text-[#4a5568]">For our clients, the most effective time to execute a series of Roth conversions is during the "Valley of Lower Income"—the period between retirement (when wage income drops) and age 73 (when large RMDs and Social Security begin).</p>
					<p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed text-[#4a5568]">During these gap years, many high-net-worth individuals find themselves in surprisingly low tax brackets (e.g., 22% or 24%). By intentionally filling up those lower brackets with Roth conversions, we move hundreds of thousands of dollars out of taxable accounts before they are subjected to higher rates later.</p>

					<h2 class="font-display mt-12 mb-4 text-3xl font-semibold text-wafs-forest">SECURE 2.0 Changes & 2025 Considerations</h2>
					<p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed text-[#4a5568]">Recent legislation has shifted the playing field:</p>
					<ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-3 pl-5 text-[#4a5568]">
						<li><strong class="font-semibold text-wafs-forest">RMD Age Pushed Back:</strong> The SECURE 2.0 Act pushed the RMD age to 73 (and eventually 75). This expands the "Valley of Lower Income," giving retirees more years to execute staggered conversions.</li>
						<li><strong class="font-semibold text-wafs-forest">The 2026 Tax Cliff:</strong> The historically low tax rates established by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 are scheduled to sunset at the end of 2025. Unless Congress acts, brackets will revert to higher historical norms (e.g., the 24% bracket jumps back to 28%). <strong class="font-semibold text-wafs-forest">This makes 2025 a highly strategic year to execute conversions.</strong></li>
					</ul>

					<h2 class="font-display mt-12 mb-4 text-3xl font-semibold text-wafs-forest">Estate Planning Synergy</h2>
					<p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed text-[#4a5568]">Beyond your own retirement, Roth conversions dramatically impact the next generation. Under the original SECURE Act, non-spouse heirs must fully drain inherited IRAs within 10 years.</p>
					<p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed text-[#4a5568]">If they inherit a Traditional IRA during their prime earning years (e.g., their 40s or 50s), those forced distributions are taxed at their highest marginal rates. If they inherit a Roth IRA, they still must drain it in 10 years, but every dollar withdrawn is tax-free.</p>

					<h2 class="font-display mt-12 mb-4 text-3xl font-semibold text-wafs-forest">When NOT to Convert</h2>
					<p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed text-[#4a5568]">Conversions are not universally beneficial. We generally advise against them if:</p>
					<ol class="mb-6 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-[#4a5568]">
						<li>You have to pay the conversion taxes out of the IRA itself (you should pay the tax with outside cash).</li>
						<li>The conversion pushes you into a significantly higher bracket or triggers Medicare IRMAA surcharges unnecessarily.</li>
						<li>You expect to be in a much lower tax bracket later in retirement.</li>
						<li>You plan to leave the Traditional IRA to charity (charities don't pay income tax on Traditional IRAs).</li>
					</ol>

					<h2 class="font-display mt-12 mb-4 text-3xl font-semibold text-wafs-forest">The WAFS Approach</h2>
					<p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed text-[#4a5568]">Roth conversions cannot be calculated on a scratchpad. We use sophisticated tax projection software to pinpoint exactly how much to convert each year—down to the dollar—to fill a tax bracket without spilling over into the next one.</p>
					<p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed text-[#4a5568]">Because our tax team and wealth team sit across the hall from one another, the CPA who signs your return and the CFP who manages your portfolio are executing the exact same playbook. <a href="/finance/contact" class="text-wafs-evergreen underline hover:text-wafs-gold">Schedule a wealth planning conversation</a> to see if a 2025 conversion makes sense for your portfolio.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</FinanceLayout>
